no store. There `evidence.read_facts` returns `empty`, `facts` is `[]`, the overall verdict is `uncovered`, and the assertion `== PR_COVERED` fails on every PR's CI run and on any other developer's clone. The sibling `test_the_v3_9_0_release_commit_on_develop_is_direct` needs no store and is fine. Fix: give the store-dependent test the `requires_local_cache` treatment that tests/test_norm_probes.py uses. First establish from an independent source that the store holds the review facts for #1231's branch, for example a fact whose `head_tree` is a tree in `09a262e0^1...09a262e0^2`. When they are missing, skip and name the missing store in the skip reason. When they are present, run the full assertion. Mark it so the marker states what it really depends on.", "files": ["tests/test_boundary_coverage.py"], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "adding_commit takes the OLDEST first-parent add of the fragment, which is wrong in the unsafe direction when a scope is re-added after a release cut", "recommendation": "instance. `pending_scopes` says the release cut removes shipped fragments, so the fragment present now was brought in by the NEWEST add on first-parent, not the oldest. Suppose a scope name is reused after a cut. `out.splitlines()[-1]` then classifies the earlier, already-shipped merge, and the current merge may read `pr-covered` when it is not. The Goals forbid that direction, and wave 2 makes this reading a gate. Take `splitlines()[0]`, the newest because log output is newest-first, and pin it with a fixture: add, delete, re-add uncovered, and assert the re-add is classified. No reuse exists in this repo's history today.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Fragments refused by pending_scopes leave the denominator silently", "recommendation": "`classify_pending` reads `pending.scopes` (valid fragments only) and ignores `pending.refusals`. A malformed pending